From: Antoni Viros <aviros@ibm.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 13:09:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Upgrade to pytorch 2.13 (#3374)

* Upgrade to pytorch 2.13

Signed-off-by: Antoni Viros i Martin <aviros@ibm.com>

* Update tests based on PT 2.13 changes for inplace ops and profiler

Signed-off-by: Antoni Viros i Martin <aviros@ibm.com>

* fix(lx): align an LX buffer's producer loop order to its consumers'

LX is per-core scratchpad, so every op touching an LX-resident buffer must
agree on which core owns which slice.  That mapping is positional:
core_to_slice_mapping() hands out core_id strides in iteration-space order,
so a producer that walks the buffer in a different dim order than its
consumers read it gets a transposed core->slice assignment.  The split
factors still multiply to the same core count, so no existing check fires --
each core simply reads the slice a different core wrote and the kernel
silently returns another core's data.

Scratchpad planning pins a graph input into LX by inserting a clone
(GraphEditor.push_allocation_with_clone), built in the buffer's natural dim
order, which need not match how the consumers read it.  Through PyTorch 2.12
Inductor's loop_ordering_after_fusion rewrote that clone into the consumers'
order, so the assignments lined up by accident.  PyTorch 2.13 refactored this
into Scheduler._try_reorder_loops_for_candidates, which still computes the
same permutation but discards it -- exposing the incoherence as wrong results
for any two reductions sharing one LX-pinned input.

torch.aminmax is the only op in the suite that naturally produces that shape
(amin + amax over one input); torch.min/max look similar but argmin/argmax
fall back to CPU, leaving a single device reduction.  On 2.13 this showed up
as 6 failures, test_aminmax_keepdim{0,1}_aminmax_pad_{2,3,4}d_dim_0.

Align the orders explicitly in a pre-fusion pass so correctness no longer
rests on an Inductor scoring heuristic.  No-op when lx_planning is off.

Verified on 2.13: the emitted SDSC bundle is byte-identical to 2.12's for
every LX case (so no performance change), aminmax 44 passed, aminmax LX
planning 88 passed, keepdim/index_copy/norm 751 passed / 12 xfailed.

Signed-off-by: Justin Turney <justin.turney@gmail.com>

* update deps to latest

Signed-off-by: Antoni Viros i Martin <aviros@ibm.com>

